                    [3] Duka paid Mr. Kondaj his statutory termination entitlements, enrolled him in its benefits program, and provided him with a T4 pursuant to ss. 57 and 75 of the <b>Employment Standards Act</b>, 2000, S.O. 2000, c. 41 (“the Act”). [4] However, neither Duka nor Crossbridge paid Mr. Kondaj any common law notice entitlements. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[<b>97</b>] Crossbridge claims that Duka’s failure to advance a crossclaim is not dispositive of costs, relying on Firth v. O’Brien, 2013 CanLII 7009 (Ont. S.C.), at para. 33. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[1] “Common law” is used only in s. 141 of the Act and “wrongful dismissal” is used only in ss. <b>97</b> and 98, which provide that an employee cannot pursue both a complaint under the Act and a claim for wrongful dismissal in relation to “the same termination or severance of employment”. 
