                <p class="snippet" >
                    In the criminal context, ambiguity must be resolved by choosing the interpretation that is most favourable to the accused: See Marcotte v. Canada (Deputy A.G.) (1974), 19 C.C.C. (2d) 257 at 261-62 (S.C.C.); R. v. Paré (1987), 38 C.C.C. (3d) <b>97</b> at 106 (S.C.C.); and R. v. McIntosh (1995), 95 C.C.C. (3d) 481 at 495 (S.C.C.). &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;An application for the review of the warrant may, nevertheless, be made to either the judge who granted the warrant or another judge of the court: R. v. Wilson (1983), 9 C.C.C. (3d) <b>97</b> (S.C.C.) at 123. In a proper case the judge may set aside the warrant. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[1] See 65302 British Columbia Ltd. v. Canada: Interpretation of the Income Tax Act, R.S.C., 1985, c. 1 (5th Supp.), s. 18(1)(a), (b), regarding whether levies, fines and penalties may be deducted as business expenses from a taxpayer's income; Rizzo &amp; Rizzo Shoes Ltd. (Re): Interpretation of the <b>Employment Standards Act</b>
                </p>
