                <p class="snippet" >
                    See also Nolan, at para. 28; Buschau v. Rogers Communications Inc., [2006] 1 S.C.R. 973, per Bastarache J., at paras. 96-<b>97</b>; Firestone Canada Inc. v. Ontario (Pension Commission) (1990), 1 O.R. (3d) 122 (C.A.), at p. 127; Huus v. Ontario (Superintendent of Pensions) (2002), 58 O.R. (3d) 380 (C.A.), at paras. 26-27. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp; also Machtinger v. HOJ Industries Ltd. (1992), 91 D.L.R. (4th) 491 (S.C.C.), at p. 507, for an analogous interpretive approach in the context of the <b>Employment Standards Act</b>, R.S.O. 1990, c. E. 14. (b) Section 69(1) [70] Section 69(1) of the Act authorizes the Superintendent to intervene in respect of a private pension &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[<b>97</b>] In Huus, this court emphasized that pension plans are created for the benefit of employees, not the companies that sponsor them. 
                </p>
